<header><p class="st-kicker">imperial city · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Free Imperial City of Dinkelsbühl</h1><p class="st-meta">1351 CE – 1802 CE · capital Dinkelsbühl</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A walled imperial city on the Wörnitz whose intact medieval fortifications make it one of Germany's best-preserved old towns.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Dinkelsbühl grew at a crossing of trade routes on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wornitz">Wörnitz</a> and received town rights under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a>. After being pledged by the crown to various lords, it secured its status as a free imperial city in 1351, when Charles IV promised that it would not be pledged again.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>In 1387 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a> won a share of power in the council. Cloth, scythe-making and trade brought prosperity, visible in the late Gothic church of Saint George. The town became mixed in religion, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-westphalia">Peace of Westphalia</a> made it one of four imperial cities where Catholics and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutherans</a> shared council offices equally.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Swedish troops occupied the town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-tyrants">the Thirty</a> Years' War, but it escaped destruction; local tradition says its children persuaded the Swedish colonel to spare it, a story celebrated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kinderzeche">Kinderzeche</a> festival. In 1802 it passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, was Prussian from 1804 to 1806 and then returned to Bavaria. Its walls, gates and houses survive almost intact.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="imperial city, 1351 CE – 1802 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V42 H32 V34 H40 V42 H46 V34 H54 V42 H60 V34 H68 V42 H76 V82 Z"/><path d="M44 82 V66 Q50 58 56 66 V82"/><path d="M34 54 H40 M60 54 H66"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">imperial city</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Germany and Central Europe</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="